WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.500
In this video, you enable the LVM add-on, turn a spare disk into an LVM volume group, and give a virtual machine a fast local volume from it.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:16.690
Under Advanced, Add-ons, open the action menu of harvester-csi-driver-lvm.

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:24.660
Select Enable. SUSE Virtualization deploys the LVM CSI driver.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:29.310
The add-on reports Deploy Successful.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:37.610
Next, give LVM a disk. On the Hosts page, open the action menu of host p5820,

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:40.670
and select Edit Config.

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:43.750
Select the Storage tab.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:46.070
Select Add Disk,

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:52.570
and choose the spare disk, /dev/sdc, one hundred twelve gigabytes.

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:57.170
Change the provisioner from Longhorn to LVM.

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:01.480
For Volume Group, select Create New.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:04.480
Name the volume group demo-vg,

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.750
and select Create.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:14.170
Select Save. SUSE Virtualization adds the disk to the new volume group.

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:16.700
Open the host,

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:19.350
and its Storage tab.

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:28.280
The disk /dev/sdc is now provisioned with LVM. Next, create a storage class on the volume group.

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:32.230
On the Storage Classes page, select Create.

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:34.660
Name it demo-lvm.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:38.380
Choose the LVM provisioner.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:46.780
LVM volumes are local, so the class is tied to one host. Choose node p5820,

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:50.560
and the volume group demo-vg.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:58.770
Keep the volume group type dm-thin. Thin volumes allocate space on write and support snapshots and clones.

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:01.230
Select Create.

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:05.640
The new storage class uses the LVM provisioner.

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:12.900
Now create a virtual machine that uses it. On the Virtual Machines page, select Create.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:16.400
Choose the namespace demo,

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:19.180
and name it demo-lvm-vm.

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:21.590
Give it one CPU

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:24.180
and two gigabytes of memory.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:26.630
On the Volumes tab,

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:31.240
pick the openSUSE Leap image for the root disk.

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:34.750
Then select Add Volume for a second disk.

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:39.150
Set its storage class to demo-lvm,

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:41.980
and its size to two gigabytes.

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:46.050
Select Create.

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:53.750
The virtual machine is scheduled on host p5820, where the volume group lives, and it is running.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:56.640
Open the virtual machine,

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.670
and its Volumes tab.

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:10.240
Below the root disk, the two gigabyte data volume comes from demo-lvm, a thin logical volume on the local disk.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:18.089
The virtual machine runs with a local LVM volume. Next, you monitor virtual machine metrics.
